The '''president of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands''' is the [[head of state]] and [[head of government]] of the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]], and heads the executive branch of the state. The office was created by the [[Constitution of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands (1927)|Constitution of 1927]], the year after independence. The president is directly elected by popular vote for a five-year renewable term and exercises executive power over the centralized state, which plans and manages public services in the manner of the [[Burawa|Burawi]] state model. The current president is [[Yohannes Bari]], in office since 2023.

== Powers and duties ==

The president manages the executive powers of the state. As the head of the executive branch, the president directs the government, appoints ministers and senior officials, and is responsible for the administration of public services, including the state-run healthcare, education, and transportation systems. The president represents the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands domestically and abroad, signs legislation into law, and acts as commander-in-chief of the armed forces.

Under the centralized state model, the presidency plans and manages the public services that the state provides to all citizens. The [[People's Assembly of Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|People's Assembly]], as the legislative branch, writes and passes laws and exercises oversight of the president and government.

== Election ==

The president is directly elected by popular vote for a term of five years. The office is renewable, and there is no concurrent requirement that the president hold a seat in the People's Assembly. Presidential elections are held separately from the biennial Assembly elections. The most recent presidential election took place in 2023, when [[Yohannes Bari]] of the [[Kadarist Party (Bolodun and Kuvari Islands)|Kadarist Party]] was elected; the next presidential election is due in 2028.

== History ==

The presidency was established by the Constitution of 1927, following independence in 1926 and the formation of the [[People's Assembly of Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|People's Assembly]]. The first president was [[Daniel Makuva]], a leading figure of the socialist movement that had carried the islands to independence, who took office following the adoption of the new constitution. The office drew on the tradition of autonomous leadership on [[Bolodun]] that had originated with the post-emancipation administration, and it has since been held by figures from across the islands' communities, reflecting the fourteen-district structure of the state. The current president, [[Yohannes Bari]], was elected in 2023.

